.TH UNIFI\-MAP 1 "2026-08-11" "unifi\-map 0.10.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
unifi\-map \- export a UniFi network topology as vector diagrams
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unifi\-map
[\fIglobal options\fR]
\fBfetch\fR|\fBrender\fR|\fBall\fR|\fBshape\fR|\fBoverrides\fR
[\fIcommand options\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The UniFi Network web interface has no topology export, and a screenshot
cannot substitute for one: the topology view is a fixed viewport wrapping a
pan and zoom canvas, so a full\-page capture returns only the visible region,
and zooming out far enough to fit the network is what makes the labels
unreadable.
.PP
\fBunifi\-map\fR reads the same JSON endpoints the interface draws from and
renders them properly: SVG and PDF that stay sharp at any zoom, PNG, Graphviz
source, draw.io files whose shapes are already positioned, Mermaid for a
README or a wiki, and JSON of the normalised topology for other programs.
.PP
It only ever reads. Every request is a GET, and there is no code path that
changes anything on the controller.
.SS Two stages
\fBfetch\fR caches the controller's JSON; \fBrender\fR turns that cache into
diagrams; \fBall\fR does both. They are separable so a map can be restyled
repeatedly without touching the controller again, and so each cached snapshot
is a record of the network at one moment.
.SS Without a controller
\fB\-\-support\-file\fR reads a console support file instead, which needs no
credentials and contacts no controller. Rendering afterwards still fetches
artwork unless \fB\-\-offline\fR or \fB\-\-icons builtin\fR is given.
.PP
A support file is not safe to pass around. It is a complete inventory of the
network: every MAC, hostname, address and lease, the SSIDs and subnets, the
WAN addresses, and logs of client activity. UniFi redacts some credential
fields by name and that pass is demonstrably incomplete. Treat one as a
secret and delete it when finished.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBfetch\fR
Cache controller data (or read \-\-support\-file instead)
.TP
\fBrender\fR
Render diagrams from cache
.TP
\fBall\fR
Fetch then render (both stages; not all formats, see \-f)
.TP
\fBshape\fR
Describe the network's shape, for sharing in an issue
.TP
\fBoverrides\fR
Check an overrides file without rendering
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Accepted before or after the subcommand, so
\fBunifi\-map all \-\-support\-file\fR \fIF\fR and
\fBunifi\-map \-\-support\-file\fR \fIF\fR \fBall\fR are equivalent.
.TP
\fB\-\-env\-file\fR
Credential file (default: $UNIFI_MAP_ENV, ./.env, ~/.config/unifi\-map/env)
.TP
\fB\-\-cache\-dir\fR
Where controller snapshots are read/written. A snapshot is a full inventory of your network, so keeping it outside a git repository is worth doing: set $UNIFI_CACHE_DIR once instead of passing this every time (default: cache)
.TP
\fB\-\-asset\-cache\fR
Where downloaded artwork is cached (default: cache/assets). Kept separate from \-\-cache\-dir so a read\-only snapshot directory stays clean.
.TP
\fB\-\-support\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read the topology from a UniFi support file (.tgz) instead of a controller. Needs no credentials and never contacts a controller. Rendering may still fetch artwork; add \-\-offline to stop that too.
.TP
\fB\-\-site\fR \fINAME\fR
Which site to read. For a live fetch this overrides UNIFI_SITE, which otherwise falls back to `default`. For a support file it picks one of the sites inside, and is required when the file holds more than one: the run stops and lists them rather than choosing for you.
.TP
\fB\-\-support\-max\-member\fR \fISIZE\fR
Largest single file to decode from a support archive (default 64M). Accepts a plain number or a K/M/G suffix. Raise it if a large site is refused.
.TP
\fB\-\-support\-max\-total\fR \fISIZE\fR
Total to decode from a support archive across all files (default 128M).
.TP
\fB\-\-support\-max\-entries\fR \fIN\fR
How many archive entries to walk before giving up (default 100000). Separate from the size caps because entry count does not follow the bytes decoded.
.TP
\fB\-\-fetch\-fingerprints\fR
Allow downloading Ubiquiti's client fingerprint database, which is what gives clients real product artwork when reading a support file. Off by default: reading a support file otherwise contacts nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-fetch\-icon\-font\fR
With \-\-support\-file, also fetch the generic client glyph font from a controller. This one DOES need UNIFI_HOST and UNIFI_API_KEY, because Ubiquiti publish no copy of that font. Off by default.
.TP
\fB\-\-icon\-font\fR \fIDIR\fR
Load the client glyph font from a directory you copied off a controller yourself (needs its style.css and .ttf). Needs no credentials and no network. See docs/artwork.md.
.TP
\fB\-\-support\-max\-archive\fR \fISIZE\fR
Total uncompressed bytes to walk in a support archive, counting files that are skipped (default 4G). This is what stops a small archive that expands enormously; the other caps only measure what is decoded.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Never show the progress spinner. It already turns itself off when output is not a terminal, so this is only needed for an interactive run whose output something else is reading.
.TP
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fR
Where diagrams are written (default: out)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Log every artwork lookup, including the ones that found nothing, and name nodes that \-\-obfuscate would otherwise hide.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
show program's version number and exit
.SH FETCH OPTIONS
\fBfetch\fR takes only the global options above.
.SH RENDER and ALL OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-offline\fR \fI{yes,no}\fR
Include devices the controller lists but that are not currently connected. Defaults to no, because a controller keeps remembering hardware long after it has been pulled from the rack; use yes when you want to see what it still thinks exists (default: no)
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-formats\fR \fI{svg,pdf,png,dot,drawio,mermaid,json,html}\fR
Output formats (default: svg drawio)
.TP
\fB\-\-icons\fR \fI{unifi,builtin}\fR
unifi: real Ubiquiti product artwork, fetched and cached at runtime, falling back to our own drawings for any node it cannot resolve, including unidentified clients when no icon font is cached. builtin: our drawings only, nothing fetched (default: unifi)
.TP
\fB\-\-layout\fR \fI{tree,unifi}\fR
unifi: left\-to\-right like the UniFi UI, no port labels. tree: top\-down and leaf\-staggered, with port labels, built to be readable on a busy network (default: unifi)
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\fR \fI{dark,light}\fR
Colour theme (default: light)
.TP
\fB\-\-transparent\fR
Draw no background, so the map sits on whatever page it is placed on. Applies to svg, pdf, png and drawio. Pick the theme to match the destination: labels are drawn straight onto the canvas with nothing behind them, so light text vanishes on a light page.
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
Never reach the network for artwork; use only what is already cached
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR
Output filename stem
Default: \fBnetwork\-map\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Overwrite output files that unifi\-map did not write. Without this, an existing .dot or .drawio it does not recognise is left alone, so a diagram you have edited by hand is not silently replaced.
.TP
\fB\-\-overrides\fR
Manual corrections: links the controller cannot see, nesting, renames, your own artwork, and hiding. Defaults to overrides.toml when that file exists
.TP
\fB\-\-obfuscate\fR
Replace hostnames, addresses, MACs, network names and SSIDs with stable placeholders, keeping topology, roles and artwork intact, so the diagram can be shared
.TP
\fB\-\-report\fR
After rendering, print a diagnostic report on stdout saying where the map came from: which endpoint placed each client, what could not be placed, and which artwork matches were refused as ambiguous. NOT safe to share, since it names your devices; use `unifi\-map shape` for that
.TP
\fB\-\-title\fR
Diagram title (default: Network map). Note that \-\-obfuscate cannot clean a title you supply yourself
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-clients\fR
Infrastructure only, no clients
.TP
\fB\-\-per\-network\fR
Also emit one diagram per client network, which keeps a busy map readable
.TP
\fB\-\-legend\fR, \fB\-\-no\-legend\fR
Show the legend (default: on for \-\-layout tree, off for \-\-layout unifi)
.TP
\fB\-\-title\-block\fR, \fB\-\-no\-title\-block\fR
Show the title and subtitle above the map. A title sets a minimum canvas width, so turning it off crops dead space on a narrow map (default: on for \-\-layout tree, off for \-\-layout unifi)
.TP
\fB\-\-stagger\fR \fIN\fR
With \-\-layout tree, stagger leaf nodes into rows of ~N to control aspect ratio (0 disables; higher is taller and narrower; default 12)
.SH SHAPE OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip the consent prompt. Read what the report contains first; `unifi\-map shape` on its own prints that and asks.
.SH OVERRIDES OPTIONS
.TP
\fBaction\fR \fI{check}\fR
check: apply the file against the cached snapshot and report, failing on any selector that matches nothing or several things
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-offline\fR \fI{yes,no}\fR
Include devices the controller lists but that are not currently connected. Defaults to no, because a controller keeps remembering hardware long after it has been pulled from the rack; use yes when you want to see what it still thinks exists (default: no)
.TP
\fB\-\-overrides\fR
Which file to check. Defaults to overrides.toml when it exists.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBUNIFI_HOST\fR
Hostname or address of the console. Required for a live fetch.
.TP
\fBUNIFI_API_KEY\fR
An API key. Required for a live fetch. A key carries the permissions of the
account that created it; UniFi offers no way to issue a read\-only one.
.TP
\fBUNIFI_SITE\fR
Which site to read. Overridden by \fB\-\-site\fR. Defaults to
\fBdefault\fR.
.TP
\fBUNIFI_VERIFY_TLS\fR
\fBtrue\fR, \fBfalse\fR, or a path to a CA bundle.
.TP
\fBUNIFI_MAP_ENV\fR
Path to a credential file, tried before the default locations.
.PP
The \fBUDM_\fR\fI*\fR spellings of these were removed in 0.9.0. A key is
never written into the environment, and is removed from the environment
Graphviz is run with.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB.env\fR, \fB~/.config/unifi\-map/env\fR
Credential files, searched in that order after \fB\-\-env\-file\fR and
\fBUNIFI_MAP_ENV\fR. Create with \fBinstall \-m 600\fR: a plain copy
inherits the umask and usually leaves an API key world\-readable.
\fBunifi\-map\fR warns when it reads one others can see.
.TP
\fBcache/\fR
Cached snapshots. A full inventory of MACs, hostnames and addresses.
Written mode 0600. Do not commit one or attach it to an issue.
.TP
\fBcache/assets/\fR
Downloaded artwork, kept apart from snapshots so a read\-only snapshot
directory stays clean.
.TP
\fBoverrides.toml\fR
Manual corrections, read automatically when present in the working
directory. See \fB\-\-overrides\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Fetch and render with the defaults:
\fBunifi\-map all\fR
.TP
Restyle without touching the controller again:
\fBunifi\-map render \-\-layout tree \-\-theme dark\fR
.TP
Map from a support file, contacting nothing at all:
\fBunifi\-map \-\-support\-file\fR \fIsupport.tgz\fR
\fBall \-\-offline\fR
.TP
Produce a diagram safe to publish:
\fBunifi\-map render \-\-obfuscate\fR
.TP
One diagram per client network:
\fBunifi\-map render \-\-per\-network\fR
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success.
.TP
\fB1\fR
The controller, a support file, Graphviz or an output path failed.
.TP
\fB2\fR
Configuration or command line error.
.TP
\fB3\fR
Graphviz is not installed. Distinct from \fB1\fR because it is fixed by
installing a package rather than by changing anything here, and because
\fBdot\fR, \fBmermaid\fR and \fBjson\fR do not need it at all.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBdot\fR(1), \fBunflatten\fR(1)
.PP
Full documentation, including the overrides format and the security notes
summarised here: \fIhttps://github.com/gitkodak/unifi\-map\fR
